A border color like `#e5e5e5` is a promise about the background. It says: this element will always sit on white. Put the same card on a tinted panel, a hover state, or dark mode, and the border either vanishes or turns into a harsh outline.

Semi-transparent borders don't make that promise. An alpha color mixes with whatever is behind it, so the border reads as "slightly darker than the surface" everywhere:

```css
.card {
  /* fixed: only correct on one background */
  border: 1px solid #e5e5e5;

  /* adaptive: correct on every background */
  border: 1px solid var(--gray-a4);
}
```

The same logic applies to dividers, input outlines, and table rules, anything that's supposed to *separate* rather than *decorate*. Radix-style color scales ship alpha variants (`--gray-a1` through `--gray-a12`) exactly for this. Reach for the alpha scale by default and save the solid values for surfaces themselves.

One bonus: alpha borders survive theme switches for free. A border that's "black at 8% opacity" is a sensible border in light mode and, flipped to "white at 8%", in dark mode, no per-theme overrides needed.

Have you ever tried to animate a container's width or height with Motion and run into this?

The one on the left just snaps to its new size. The text animates in fine, but the container itself jumps. That's because width and height are not animatable. The browser doesn't know how to interpolate between a fixed value and "whatever the content needs."

The one on the right is way smoother and feels better. Same content change, but the width animates to fit. So how do we achieve this?

# Building a useMeasure Hook

Before we get into the animation part, we need a way to track an element's dimensions. The browser has a native API for this called `ResizeObserver`.[^1] It watches an element and fires a callback whenever its size changes. We can wrap this in a small hook.

```tsx

function useMeasure() {
  const [element, setElement] = useState(null);
  const [bounds, setBounds] = useState({ width: 0, height: 0 });

  const ref = useCallback((node) => {
    setElement(node);
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!element) return;

    const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
      setBounds({
        width: entry.contentRect.width,
        height: entry.contentRect.height,
      });
    });

    observer.observe(element);
    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, [element]);

  return [ref, bounds];
}
```

The hook returns a `ref` callback and a `bounds` object. Attach the ref to any element and `bounds` will always reflect its current width and height. When the content inside that element changes and causes a resize, the observer fires, state updates, and your component re-renders with the new values.

There are libraries like [react-use-measure](https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-use-measure) that do this too, but as you can see it's only a few lines of code. No dependency needed.[^2]

# Integrating with Motion

Now for the animation. The core idea is two divs. An outer div that you animate, and an inner div that you measure. Attach the `ref` from `useMeasure` to the inner div, the hook gives you `bounds.width` and `bounds.height` which update whenever the content changes, pass those values to Motion's `animate` prop on the outer div.

```tsx

function AnimatedContainer({ children }) {
  const [ref, bounds] = useMeasure();

  return (
    <motion.div animate={{ height: bounds.height }}>
      <div ref={ref}>{children}</div>
    </motion.div>
  );
}
```

# Animating Width

Buttons that change their label are a common use case. Loading states, confirmation messages, multi-step forms. Without animated bounds the button jumps between widths. With this pattern, it slides.

The trick here is the ref goes on the inner wrapper, not the button itself. The button's width is controlled by Motion. The wrapper's width is controlled by its content. When the content changes, the measured width updates, and the button smoothly resizes to fit.

One thing to note is that I'm checking `bounds.width > 0` before animating. This avoids an animation from 0 on the initial render. You want the first frame to just show the button at its natural size, not animate in from nothing.

# Animating Height

Height is where this pattern really shines. Expandable sections, accordions, FAQs, details panels. Anywhere content reveals or hides itself.

The same pattern applies. Measure the inner content, animate the outer container.

When items are added or removed, the measured height changes and the animation follows. Works for any dynamic content, text, images, lists, nested components.

# Gotchas

On initial render, `bounds.width` and `bounds.height` will be 0 before the first measurement. Guard against this or you'll get an animation from 0 to the actual size on mount. A simple `bounds.height > 0 ? bounds.height : "auto"` works.

Don't measure and animate the same element. The ref goes on the inner div, the `animate` prop goes on the outer div. If you put both on the same element you create a loop, the animation changes the size, which triggers a new measurement, which triggers a new animation.

Add a small delay to the animation to give the feel of a natural transition catching up to the content.

Finally, don't overuse this pattern. It's a subtle effect that should be used sparingly. Use it when it makes sense, like for buttons, accordions, or other interactive elements.

[^1]: [ResizeObserver](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/ResizeObserver) is supported in all modern browsers. It watches for changes to an element's content or border box size without causing layout thrashing.

[^2]: [react-use-measure](https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-use-measure) is a popular alternative maintained by the [Poimandres](https://github.com/pmndrs) team. It adds some extras like debouncing and scroll tracking, but for most cases the hook above is all you need.

You've seen the broken version a thousand times, a heading that runs long, wraps, and leaves a single word stranded on the second line. Typographers call it an orphan, and it makes even careful designs look accidental.

The old fixes were all bad. Manual `<br>` tags break at every viewport width except the one you tested. JavaScript balancers reflow text after paint. Non-breaking spaces between the last two words help sometimes and fail silently other times.

Now it's one property:

```css
h1, h2, h3 {
  text-wrap: balance;
}
```

The browser redistributes the words so every line comes out roughly equal, at whatever width the heading happens to render.

Balance is for headings, browsers only apply it to a handful of lines because the layout cost grows with line count. For body text, use `text-wrap: pretty` instead, which is cheaper and focuses just on preventing orphans. Set both once in your base styles and forget about them.

```css
h1, h2, h3 { text-wrap: balance; }
p { text-wrap: pretty; }
```

Put a rounded card inside a rounded card and give both the same border radius. It looks subtly off, and most people can't say why. The corners feel pinched, like the inner card is fighting the outer one.

The reason is geometry. For two curves to look concentric, they need to share a center point. When there's padding between the elements, the inner corner sits closer to that center, so it needs a *smaller* radius. The rule is simple:

```css
.outer {
  --padding: 8px;
  --inner-radius: 8px;

  border-radius: calc(var(--inner-radius) + var(--padding));
  padding: var(--padding);
}

.inner {
  border-radius: var(--inner-radius);
}
```

Inner radius equals outer radius minus the gap. Or worked the other way, outer equals inner plus padding. Either direction works, as long as the difference between the two radii is exactly the padding between them.

Once you see it, you can't unsee it. iOS does this everywhere, app icons inside folders, cards inside sheets. It's one calc away, so wire it up with custom properties once and every nested surface in your interface inherits corners that actually agree with each other.

The Doherty threshold says interactions need to respond within 400 milliseconds to feel instant.[^1] Most mutations, a like, a follow, a settings toggle, take longer than that round-trip. So the honest version of the UI shows a spinner, and the honest version feels broken.

Optimistic UI flips the order. Update the interface immediately, fire the request in the background, and roll back in the rare case it fails:

```tsx
async function handleLike() {
  setLiked(true); // respond now

  try {
    await api.like(post.id); // confirm later
  } catch {
    setLiked(false); // roll back on failure
  }
}
```

The waiting version isn't slower on the network, it's identical. It just makes you watch. The optimistic version feels like the button is an extension of your hand, which is the entire point.

The judgment call is confidence. Optimism fits actions that almost always succeed and are cheap to undo, toggles, likes, reorders. It doesn't fit payments or destructive operations, where showing work honestly *is* the right feel. And when you genuinely can't be optimistic, fake the speed another way: skeletons and progress indicators exist because perceived speed matters as much as actual speed.

[^1]: Doherty, W. J., & Kelisky, R. P. (1979). Managing VM/CMS systems for user effectiveness. *IBM Systems Journal*, 18(1), 143-163.
